所以我正在做一个比较研究，对象是沿着H街行走的人，以及边走边互相交谈的人。所以两对听力正常的人，头上戴着同样的摄像头，然后两对聋人，头上也安装了这种摄像头。Diad（二元组）只是社会学中指代一对人的一个花哨术语。所以罗伯特给这两对人（听力正常和聋人）戴上了摄像头，让他们沿着街道走。

7
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:51.000
And he did this so he could precisely monitor what people look at when they walk and talk, versus when they walk and sign. And he found very clear differences. To hearing people look straight ahead the whole time they were walking, you might see just a few seconds, very rare seconds, when the person would look at the other person who they were walking beside and speaking. They wouldn't ever make eye contact.
他这样做是为了精确监测人们在边走边聊时，与边走边打手语时所看的地方。他发现了非常明显的差异。听力正常的人在走路时一直直视前方，你可能只会看到几秒钟，非常罕见的几秒钟，当那个人会看向他身边一起走路并交谈的另一个人。他们从不进行眼神交流。

8
00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:14.000
One would look at the other. Well, as with deaf people, there was a continuous maintained eye contact. So because American sign language is a visual language, it requires constant eye contact. But maintaining eye contact also means you can't pay full attention to any obstacles up ahead. And that's a problem if you're walking. I mean I guess if you can think about yourself as a person you can hear. If you weren't looking at the road as you were
一个人会看向另一个人。然而，对于聋人来说，有持续保持的眼神交流。因为美国手语是一种视觉语言，它需要持续的眼神交流。但保持眼神交流也意味着你无法完全注意前方可能出现的障碍物。如果你在走路，这就是个问题。我的意思是，我想如果你把自己想象成一个能听见的人。如果你在开车时没有看路，

9
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:50.000
driving and looking at your passenger, as you're driving down the road, you can imagine how difficult that might be to mediate your car down the road. Well the same kind of things happens with deaf people, but we watch out for one another. For walking and signing to work, the people holding a conversation need to keep an eye out for obstacles. But it's not like deaf people make a deal at the beginning of a conversation, you talk, I'll be the lookout. When deaf people are looking at one another, there's an agreement, an internal agreement, unspoken agreement that's understood between the two people conversing. It's almost like the people when you're in a conversation, you're in a kind of dance, where you're not discussing what's happening between each other,
而是在开车时看着你的乘客，你可以想象在路上驾驶汽车有多困难。嗯，聋人身上也发生了同样的事情，但我们会互相照看。为了让边走边打手语行得通，进行对话的人需要留意障碍物。但这并不是说聋人在对话开始时会达成协议，你说话，我来负责观察。当聋人互相注视时，有一种默契，一种内在的协议，一种在交谈的两人之间心照不宣的协议。这几乎就像人们在交谈时，处于一种舞蹈中，你们不是在讨论彼此之间正在发生什么，

10
00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:14.000
but you just sort of know what the next move should be. That's exactly it. When you're dancing, you kind of work with your partner in the way they breathe, the way they move their body, and you respond accordingly, the same thing holds true with deaf people in a conversation. When they're designing buildings, most hearing architects are not thinking about providing a venue for this dance. Robert says a lot of spaces are too narrow and confining for a good conversation.
而是你只是某种程度上知道下一步该做什么。正是这样。当你跳舞时，你会根据舞伴的呼吸方式、身体移动方式与他们配合，并做出相应的反应，聋人在交谈时也是如此。在设计建筑物时，大多数听力正常的建筑师并没有考虑为这种舞蹈提供场地。罗伯特说，很多空间太狭窄和局促，无法进行良好的对话。

11
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:32.000
There's this sense that buildings were not designed in such a way as if they accommodate deaf people. So how does that feel? I guess the best way to say that is like we feel like sardines. You know, like we're all lined up one after the next and we're trying to converse in that way and there's no ease in our communication. It's actually also true of many buildings
有一种感觉是，建筑的设计并没有考虑到聋人的需求。那感觉如何？我想最好的说法是，我们感觉像沙丁鱼。你知道，就像我们一个接一个地排成一排，试图以那种方式交谈，而我们的交流并不轻松。这实际上也适用于许多建筑物，

12
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:54.000
at Gallaudet University, even though it's dedicated to teaching the deaf and hard of hearing. It's a relatively old campus. A lot of buildings were built in the mid 1800s and it looks like a typical northeastern liberal arts college. But there are signs that this school is different than your Wesleyans and your Amhersts or whatever. Take for example the doorbells on campus.
在加劳德特大学，尽管它致力于教授聋人和听障人士。这是一个相对古老的校园。许多建筑建于19世纪中期，看起来像典型的东北部文理学院。但有迹象表明，这所学校与你的卫斯理大学、阿默斯特学院或其他学校不同。以校园里的门铃为例。

13
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Nowadays people use lights, you can flick a light switch and let somebody know that you're at the door, but in the 1800s before electric light they used vibration. The university has a display of an old doorbell. It's about four feet high with a round metal handle attached to a chain. And at the other end of that chain is a lead weight. You'd arrive at the door and you'd simply pull the chain. With that hit to the floor, I mean we're listening to the chain, but that hit to the floor, you'd immediately get it, someone's at the door.
如今人们使用灯光，你可以拨动电灯开关让某人知道你在门口，但在1800年代电灯出现之前，他们使用振动。大学里有一个旧门铃的展示。它大约四英尺高，有一个连接在链条上的圆形金属把手。链条的另一端是一个铅坠。你到达门口，只需拉动链条。随着那东西撞击地板，我的意思是我们在听链条的声音，但那撞击地板的声音，你会立刻明白，有人在门口。

14
00:05:29.000 --> 00:06:02.000
That's Hansel Bauman. He is now the director of design and planning a Gallaudet. Bauman is not deaf, and in this case that is his voice. And this was one of the really cool, but kind of a necronomistic innovations at Gallaudet. But several years ago, the university realized that some of its standards of architecture and innovation had slipped. So they asked Hansel to help lead a group project that would rethink the campus design with deaf people in mind. They called the project Deaf Space. Deaf Space really started at the beginning of time whenever there are deaf people there is
那是汉塞尔·鲍曼。他现在是加劳德特设计与规划的主任。鲍曼不是聋人，在这种情况下，那是他的声音。这是加劳德特非常酷但有点时代错乱的创新之一。但几年前，大学意识到其建筑和创新的一些标准已经下滑。所以他们请汉塞尔领导一个小组项目，重新思考以聋人为核心的校园设计。他们把这个项目称为“聋人空间”。聋人空间实际上从时间之初就开始了，无论哪里有聋人，就有

15
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:32.000
this idea of Deaf Space. Deaf people have sensibilities of adapting their world to their way of being. The campus took those ideas and poured them into the design of a new building called the Sorenson Language and Communication Center, but everyone calls it the SLCC. It was built in 2008, and they're also working on a new dorm. Hansel Bauman showed me around, and I saw that there are a lot of little innovations. Some that come to mind immediately like the type of doors.
这种“聋人空间”的理念。聋人有将世界适应他们生存方式的敏感性。校园采用了这些想法，并将其融入到一座名为索伦森语言与交流中心的新建筑设计中，但每个人都称它为SLCC。它建于2008年，他们也在筹建一个新的宿舍。汉塞尔·鲍曼带我参观了一圈，我看到有很多小创新。有些立刻浮现在脑海中，比如门的类型。

16
00:06:32.000 --> 00:07:09.000
If you're walking and signing a normal door will stop your conversation cold. You notice we just passed through two sliding doors. The sliding doors are purposely designed to allow people to continue, assign the conversation as they enter the building so you don't stop, open the door, start the conversation again. And then there are more subtle changes. One of the big ideas behind Deaf Space is avoiding eye strain. That's because when you communicate all day with your eyes, it's easy to get tired. So if you have bad lighting conditions, for example,
如果你在走路并打手语，一扇普通的门会立刻中断你的谈话。你注意到我们刚刚穿过了两扇感应门。感应门是专门设计的，允许人们在进入建筑物时继续打手语对话，这样你就不用停下来、开门、然后再开始对话。还有一些更微妙的变化。“聋人空间”背后的一个大理念是避免眼睛疲劳。这是因为当你整天用眼睛交流时，很容易感到疲倦。所以如果你有糟糕的照明条件，例如，

17
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:28.000
which is backlighting, glare, high contrast between dark spaces and light spaces, there are conditions in the environment which tend to visually vibrate. Like if you have horizontal blinds and light coming through the blinds, you'll start getting vibration. All of those annoyances in and of themselves may not be so bad, but taken together
也就是背光、眩光、黑暗空间和明亮空间之间的高对比度，环境中存在往往会产生视觉振动的条件。比如如果你有百叶窗，光线透过百叶窗射入，你就会开始感到振动。所有这些烦恼本身可能并不算太糟，但加在一起，

18
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:48.000
over the time of the day, it's the beginning of eye strain. And eye strain really wears people out, basically. I've actually noticed that myself, like when I look at horizontal blinds and get that sort of eye vibration, and I thought I was just weird. I don't think you're weird, Tom. So the Sorenson building avoids those traps.
在一天的时间里，这就是眼睛疲劳的开始。眼睛疲劳基本上会让人精疲力竭。我实际上自己也注意到了，比如当我看着百叶窗并产生那种眼睛振动时，我以为我只是很奇怪。我不认为你很奇怪，汤姆。所以索伦森大楼避免了这些陷阱。

19
00:07:48.000 --> 00:08:24.000
It has enormous windows and a lot of diffuse light to reduce shadows. While you have some direct light coming in, it's still kind of filled with this diffuse quality of light that there's really not a lot of shadows on the face. There's not a lot of distraction on surfaces behind us. And when we walked by a classroom, Hansel pointed inside at the color scheme. The color of the blue classroom, that's a color we've been experimenting with, is a good contrast to a range of skin tones. You can just so clearly see sign language in there, and that would be better when you say you can the next door.
它有巨大的窗户和大量的漫射光来减少阴影。虽然有一些直射光射入，但它仍然充满了这种漫射光质量，脸上真的没有太多阴影。我们身后的表面也没有太多干扰。当我们走过一间教室时，汉塞尔指着里面的配色方案。蓝色教室的颜色，那是我们一直在试验的颜色，与各种肤色形成了很好的对比。你可以非常清楚地看到那里的手语，当你提到隔壁时，那会更好。

