00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:48.000
Not all of the changes worked. In the building's design they decided to round off sharp corners. That's because if you can't hear footsteps from around the corner, a lot of times you won't know someone's coming at all and people bump into each other. So they rounded off these corners. Again, a lesson learned. We realized that people now hug that curved corner like a race car and you still kind of have that problem.
并非所有的改变都奏效。在建筑设计中，他们决定将尖角磨圆。这是因为如果你听不到拐角处的脚步声，很多时候你根本不知道有人要过来，人们会撞在一起。所以他们把这些拐角磨圆了。同样，这是一个教训。我们意识到人们现在像赛车一样贴着那个弯曲的拐角走，你仍然会遇到那种问题。

21
00:08:48.000 --> 00:09:16.000
So our strategy now is to create glass corners that allow you to kind of see through the corner, but it still extend that period of time that you have before you get to the corner. The building is definitely pleasant to look at. The color scheme is nice, the lightest pleasing, but as someone who can hear, I probably would not have noticed a lot of these little changes if Hansel hadn't pointed them out. But after walking around with Hansel, the most striking thing about the building
所以我们现在的策略是创造玻璃拐角，让你能够透过拐角看，但这仍然延长了你在到达拐角之前的时间。这座建筑看起来确实令人愉悦。配色方案很好，光线令人满意，但作为一个能听见的人，如果汉塞尔没有指出，我可能不会注意到很多这些小变化。但在与汉塞尔走了一圈后，关于这座建筑最引人注目的事情

22
00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:43.000
was the level of thoughtfulness. Everything in the building seems to have a specific thought behind it. Chairs are on wheels so you can constantly rearrange them in circles, and they made benches out of wood because you can feel vibrations more easily through the wood. And they try to strategically place air conditioning units so they don't make as much noise. That's because it turns out that hearing aids can pick up that sound in a really jarring way.
是那种体贴的程度。建筑里的每件事似乎都有特定的想法。椅子装有轮子，这样你可以不断地把它们重新排列成圆圈，他们用木头制作长椅，因为你可以更容易地通过木头感觉到振动。他们试图战略性地放置空调装置，这样它们就不会发出太大的噪音。这是因为事实证明，助听器会以一种非常刺耳的方式接收到那种声音。

23
00:09:43.000 --> 00:10:12.000
And for Hansel, working on deaf space has meant becoming sensitive to a range of issues that he had never thought of before. I think the window that the deaf experience offers to architects is to provide us with a new kind of awareness around what's working and what's not working in a room. That we might accept every day is just kind of our condition and we're not aware of it. It's not in front of us in a way that we can name what's the
对于汉塞尔来说，从事“聋人空间”的工作意味着要对一系列他以前从未想过的议题变得敏感。我认为聋人体验为建筑师提供的窗口是，让我们对房间里什么有效、什么无效有了新的认识。我们可能每天都接受它作为我们的某种状况，而我们并没有意识到它。它不是以一种我们可以命名的方式出现在我们面前，

24
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:52.000
aggregation in the room or the environmental stressor. We don't have a way to recognize that, whereas I think it's part of every day deaf experience. That's why everyone involved in the deaf space Project says it is not just for Deaf people. Fundamentally, this is about universal design. Designed for the widest range of people possible with a variety of abilities, because not only is it more inclusive, it's demonstrably better design, regardless of need. When AXO and Smart Design collaborated on making a potato peeler for people with arthritis, they created a new peeler with an ergonomic, big, fat black handle that turned out to be better for everyone. Now you see that application everywhere.
房间里的聚集或环境压力源。我们没有办法识别这一点，而我认为这是聋人日常体验的一部分。这就是为什么参与“聋人空间”项目的每个人都说它不仅仅是为了聋人。从根本上说，这是关于通用设计。为具有各种能力的最广泛的人群进行设计，因为这不仅更具包容性，而且无论需求如何，它都是明显更好的设计。当AXO和Smart Design合作制造一种为关节炎患者使用的削皮器时，他们创造了一种带有符合人体工程学的、大而肥厚的黑色手柄的新削皮器，结果证明这对每个人都更好。现在你到处都能看到这种应用。

